1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,348
Everything in this section so far has assumed you're building the feature and needed

2
00:00:05,348 --> 00:00:06,359
to build it well.

3
00:00:06,359 --> 00:00:10,999
This lecture assumes something different: that the most valuable thing you can

4
00:00:10,999 --> 00:00:12,725
decide, sometimes, is not to.

5
00:00:12,725 --> 00:00:16,116
Nobody gets promoted for the AI feature they didn't ship.

6
00:00:16,116 --> 00:00:20,043
So who in the room is actually going to argue for not building it?

7
00:00:20,043 --> 00:00:24,862
That's exactly why this checklist matters: the pressure is always toward building

8
00:00:24,862 --> 00:00:29,859
something, and the discipline you need is the same one from the rest of this course,

9
00:00:29,859 --> 00:00:33,191
pointed at the decision itself instead of at the output.

10
00:00:33,191 --> 00:00:34,083
Five questions.

11
00:00:34,083 --> 00:00:37,236
If you answer yes to any of them, that's not a maybe.

12
00:00:37,236 --> 00:00:42,056
That's a specific, nameable reason to stop, or to fix, before you write a line of

13
00:00:42,056 --> 00:00:42,353
spec.

14
00:00:42,353 --> 00:00:45,030
This lecture is marked hands-on for a reason.

15
00:00:45,030 --> 00:00:47,410
Reading a checklist teaches you nothing.

16
00:00:47,410 --> 00:00:52,229
Running it against something you actually care about shipping is the only version

17
00:00:52,229 --> 00:00:54,311
that changes what you do next week.

18
00:00:54,861 --> 00:00:56,155
Here's the checklist.

19
00:00:56,155 --> 00:00:59,727
Five questions, and one yes is enough to stop and rethink.

20
00:00:59,727 --> 00:01:04,901
Severe and irreversible, with nothing catching it first, that's the act archetype at

21
00:01:04,901 --> 00:01:08,782
its most dangerous, last lecture's chart telling you the truth.

22
00:01:08,782 --> 00:01:13,586
Needing correctness every time, not just usually, is asking the mechanism from

23
00:01:13,586 --> 00:01:17,344
lecture one to do something it fundamentally isn't built for.

24
00:01:17,344 --> 00:01:22,271
Ungroundable and unverifiable together means neither of hallucination's two real

25
00:01:22,271 --> 00:01:25,474
fixes is available to you, so you're flying on hope.

26
00:01:25,474 --> 00:01:30,648
Volume too low means the golden set from Section four costs more than the problem is

27
00:01:30,648 --> 00:01:31,018
worth.

28
00:01:31,018 --> 00:01:36,007
And if a simple rule already solves it, adding a probabilistic system on top only

29
00:01:36,007 --> 00:01:38,655
adds risk to a feature that already worked.

30
00:01:38,655 --> 00:01:43,398
Read them again and notice none of the five asks whether the model is capable

31
00:01:43,398 --> 00:01:43,829
enough.

32
00:01:43,829 --> 00:01:48,942
Every one of them is a question about your situation: what happens when it's wrong,

33
00:01:48,942 --> 00:01:53,253
whether you can check it, whether the volume justifies the investment.

34
00:01:53,253 --> 00:01:54,362
That's deliberate.

35
00:01:54,362 --> 00:01:55,840
Capability keeps moving.

36
00:01:55,840 --> 00:01:58,551
Your situation is what you actually control.

37
00:01:59,101 --> 00:02:03,862
Here's the same five questions laid out as a path, because that's how it actually

38
00:02:03,862 --> 00:02:05,037
plays out in a room.

39
00:02:05,037 --> 00:02:09,799
One yes, on any single gate, and you stop right there and name what has to change

40
00:02:09,799 --> 00:02:11,092
before you go further.

41
00:02:11,092 --> 00:02:15,853
Only a no on every one of them clears you through to the far side, and even then,

42
00:02:15,853 --> 00:02:20,614
you land on the eval work last lecture's chart said this archetype demands, not a

43
00:02:20,614 --> 00:02:21,202
free pass.

44
00:02:21,752 --> 00:02:26,536
Same company, same underlying model, two completely different verdicts, and this is

45
00:02:26,536 --> 00:02:28,093
the exercise, run for real.

46
00:02:28,093 --> 00:02:30,226
The ticket summariser passes cleanly.

47
00:02:30,226 --> 00:02:34,606
A wrong summary gets caught by the agent reading it before anything happens.

48
00:02:34,606 --> 00:02:38,526
It's groundable in the actual ticket text and verifiable against it.

49
00:02:38,526 --> 00:02:42,849
And ticket volume is high enough that building a golden set pays for itself

50
00:02:42,849 --> 00:02:43,541
immediately.

51
00:02:43,541 --> 00:02:48,326
Auto-approving refunds over five hundred dollars fails on at least two questions at

52
00:02:48,326 --> 00:02:48,614
once.

53
00:02:48,614 --> 00:02:52,764
Money leaves before any human checks it, that's severe and irreversible.

54
00:02:52,764 --> 00:02:57,376
And it needs to be right every single time, not usually, because "usually" means

55
00:02:57,376 --> 00:03:00,834
real dollars walking out the door on a predictable schedule.

56
00:03:00,834 --> 00:03:05,273
Notice these are the same seven archetypes from last lecture, just met at the

57
00:03:05,273 --> 00:03:07,636
decision point instead of after the fact.

58
00:03:07,636 --> 00:03:10,230
Summarise sits at the safe end of that chart.

59
00:03:10,230 --> 00:03:11,959
Act sits at the dangerous end.

60
00:03:11,959 --> 00:03:16,167
The checklist is what that chart feels like before you've built anything.

61
00:03:16,717 --> 00:03:21,266
Say the honest caveat, because "no" can sound bigger than it actually is.

62
00:03:21,266 --> 00:03:25,254
The refund example didn't fail because AI can never touch money.

63
00:03:25,254 --> 00:03:30,364
It failed because right now, today, it isn't groundable enough, verifiable enough,

64
00:03:30,364 --> 00:03:31,548
or reviewed enough.

65
00:03:31,548 --> 00:03:36,346
Add a human approval step back in for anything over five hundred dollars, and

66
00:03:36,346 --> 00:03:38,091
question one stops applying.

67
00:03:38,091 --> 00:03:42,204
Build enough volume and history, and question four stops applying.

68
00:03:42,204 --> 00:03:45,320
A disqualification is a punch list, not a verdict.

69
00:03:45,320 --> 00:03:50,554
It tells you exactly what would have to change, which is far more useful than either

70
00:03:50,554 --> 00:03:52,423
a blind yes or a permanent no.

71
00:03:52,973 --> 00:03:57,660
That's the whole tool, and notice it's designed to run before the spec, not after

72
00:03:57,660 --> 00:04:00,264
something's already gone wrong in production.

73
00:04:00,264 --> 00:04:04,835
The exercise is blunt: run all five, right now, against the real feature you're

74
00:04:04,835 --> 00:04:07,844
closest to, and do it with someone else in the room.

75
00:04:07,844 --> 00:04:12,357
If you hit silence on any question, nobody actually knows the answer, and that

76
00:04:12,357 --> 00:04:14,845
silence is exactly as informative as a yes.

77
00:04:14,845 --> 00:04:17,507
It means find out before you build, not after.

78
00:04:18,057 --> 00:04:19,665
One thing before you move on.

79
00:04:19,665 --> 00:04:23,934
Run the five questions against whatever you're closest to building right now.

80
00:04:23,934 --> 00:04:25,764
Not hypothetically, the real one.

81
00:04:25,764 --> 00:04:30,144
For every yes you get, write down, specifically, what would have to become true

82
00:04:30,144 --> 00:04:31,531
before that answer flips.

83
00:04:31,531 --> 00:04:36,188
That list is worth more than the checklist itself, because it turns a stop sign into

84
00:04:36,188 --> 00:04:36,743
a roadmap.

85
00:04:36,743 --> 00:04:41,234
Next lecture closes out this section: what you've actually built here, and how it

86
00:04:41,234 --> 00:04:43,785
plugs into everything from Section two onward.
